WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.563
Hello!

2
00:00:00.563 --> 00:00:09.853
Today we study linear equations of second and higher order whose coefficients are constant numbers.

3
00:00:09.853 --> 00:00:16.609
Such equations appear in electric circuits, springs and control systems.

4
00:00:16.609 --> 00:00:26.930
By the end of the lesson you will be able to write the general solution of any such equation in a few minutes,

5
00:00:26.930 --> 00:00:30.590
by solving just one algebraic equation.

6
00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:33.327
The key idea is very simple.

7
00:00:33.327 --> 00:00:37.591
We look for a solution of the form e to the r x.

8
00:00:37.591 --> 00:00:44.964
Each derivative only adds a factor r: the first derivative is r times e to the r x,

9
00:00:44.964 --> 00:00:47.895
the second is r squared times it.

10
00:00:47.895 --> 00:00:56.423
Substituting into the equation and cancelling the non-zero exponential leaves a polynomial in r.

11
00:00:56.423 --> 00:00:59.798
This is the characteristic polynomial.

12
00:01:00.048 --> 00:01:02.698
Roots come in three kinds.

13
00:01:02.698 --> 00:01:07.386
Each distinct real root gives one exponential.

14
00:01:07.386 --> 00:01:15.437
A repeated root, say a double one, gives e to the r x and x times e to the r x.

15
00:01:15.437 --> 00:01:26.342
A complex pair alpha plus or minus i beta gives e to the alpha x times cosine beta x and times sine beta x.

16
00:01:26.592 --> 00:01:32.687
Example: y double prime minus two y prime plus five y equals zero,

17
00:01:32.687 --> 00:01:38.412
with y of zero equal to one and y prime of zero equal to five.

18
00:01:38.412 --> 00:01:45.337
The characteristic equation is r squared minus two r plus five equals zero.

19
00:01:45.337 --> 00:01:54.110
The discriminant is negative, minus sixteen, so the roots are complex: one plus or minus two i.

20
00:01:54.360 --> 00:02:02.495
So alpha is one and beta is two, and the general solution is e to the x times, in brackets,

21
00:02:02.495 --> 00:02:06.161
C one cosine two x plus C two sine two x.

22
00:02:06.161 --> 00:02:15.548
At x equal to zero the cosine is one and the sine is zero, so the first condition gives C one equals one.

23
00:02:15.548 --> 00:02:21.806
For the second condition we first differentiate with the product rule.

24
00:02:22.056 --> 00:02:26.940
The derivative at zero equals C one plus two C two.

25
00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:30.484
It must be five, so C two equals two.

26
00:02:30.484 --> 00:02:37.475
The answer is y equals e to the x times cosine two x plus two sine two x.

27
00:02:37.475 --> 00:02:46.190
Check: substituting into the equation gives zero, and the initial values are right as well.

28
00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:48.015
Look at the graph.

29
00:02:48.015 --> 00:02:52.129
The first curve is our solution: it oscillates,

30
00:02:52.129 --> 00:02:57.905
but its amplitude grows like e to the x because alpha is positive.

31
00:02:57.905 --> 00:03:07.532
The second curve is the case of negative alpha, e to the minus x times cosine two x: the oscillation dies out.

32
00:03:07.532 --> 00:03:14.534
The sign of alpha decides growth or decay, and beta sets how fast it oscillates.

33
00:03:14.784 --> 00:03:16.428
Now test yourself.

34
00:03:16.428 --> 00:03:26.842
Answer the question on the screen: which functions solve y double prime plus four y prime plus four y equals zero?

35
00:03:26.842 --> 00:03:34.059
Write the characteristic polynomial and find its roots with their multiplicity.

36
00:03:34.059 --> 00:03:37.622
Pause the video and choose your answer.
